{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "_comment": "Flooring types. Each names a generator in lib/flooring.js and supplies its options. Add an entry here and it appears in the room panel's finish list with no code change. 'script' generators run a JS body returning scene nodes — see samples/flooring-script-examples.json for worked examples.",
  "_reflectance_comment": "`reflectance` is the share of light the surface throws back, 0..1, and it is a real physical property: polished white marble is around 0.65, mid oak 0.25, black granite 0.05, grass 0.08. `lighting.js` credits it through `lighting.bounce`, so two identical lamps over Statuario and over black granite stop being the same amount of usable light. A type that omits it reflects nothing. Any room can override it in its own panel, and `solid` starts at 0 so a flat colour behaves exactly as a flat colour until you say otherwise. Stock values are approximate visual-model defaults, not measured product specifications; override them for a known material.",
  "fallback": "plain",
  "_generatorOptions_comment": "What each generator in lib/flooring.js actually READS, so the flooring editor can offer exactly those fields instead of keeping its own copy of this list — a hand-kept table in the editor is the thing that goes stale the first time a generator gains an option. `kind` picks the control: color (a #hex or an @token), number, angle, fraction (0..1). The suite asserts every generator the renderer has is described here and that nothing here names an option its generator never reads.",
